A libfprint driver for the Xiaomi Pad 8 Pro side fingerprint reader
==================================================================

The reader is a Goodix sensor driven from the secure world. Images and
templates never reach Linux: this side starts an operation and then asks the
trusted application what it made of each finger. That makes it a match-on-chip
driver with device storage.

What is here
------------

  gfpia-tee.c/h   Talking to a Qualcomm trusted application: opening the
                  qcomtee device, reaching the QSEECom compatibility loader,
                  reassembling a split MDT image, and carrying a request and
                  its payload across. Lifted unchanged from the program it was
                  proven with, qcomtee/qcomtee-qseecom.c.

  gfpia.c/h       The fingerprint protocol. Nothing in it blocks: poll the
                  descriptor gfpia_irq_fd() returns and call gfpia_handle_irq()
                  when it is readable, which is what lets the driver live
                  inside libfprint's main loop.

  xiaomi-piano.c  The FpDevice itself.

  gfpia-test.c    Exercises gfpia.c on its own, without libfprint. Worth
                  reaching for first: a fault reproduced here takes two presses
                  rather than the thirty-five a full enrolment needs.

The protocol, and where every number in it came from, is in
qcomtee/goodix-gfpia.md.

What it needs at runtime
------------------------

  /dev/tee0                   the qcomtee driver
  /dev/uioN                   the xiaomi-fp-uio module, from kernel/;
                              found by the name in /sys/class/uio/uioN/name,
                              not by a fixed minor
  qcomtee-fs-supplicant       the application's storage, running
  /lib/firmware/qcom/sm8750/xiaomi/piano-fp/
                              the application's split image, plus the requests
                              and payloads that initialise it

The firmware directory holds gfpia.mdt and gfpia.b00..b08 alongside
request-NNNN.bin and payload-NNNN.bin for 1000, 1001, six 1113 model chunks,
1005 and 1014. The payloads carry Goodix's sensor configuration and both
algorithm models, so they are shipped rather than built.

Building it into libfprint
--------------------------

Copy this directory to libfprint/drivers/xiaomi-piano/ and add it to the
driver table in libfprint/meson.build:

    'xiaomi_piano' : files(
        'drivers/xiaomi-piano/xiaomi-piano.c',
        'drivers/xiaomi-piano/gfpia.c',
        'drivers/xiaomi-piano/gfpia-tee.c',
    ),

then

    meson setup build --prefix=/usr --libdir=lib \
        -Dintrospection=false -Ddoc=false -Dgtk-examples=false \
        -Dinstalled-tests=false -Ddrivers=xiaomi_piano
    ninja -C build && ninja -C build install

libfprint 1.94.100 needs one fix to configure under meson 1.12: in
tests/meson.build, `foreach driver_test: drivers_tests` iterates a dict and
has to become `foreach driver_test, _ : drivers_tests`.

install.sh does all of the above. It writes straight into /usr, which is what
makes it useful while working on the driver - but it leaves pacman's idea of
libfprint wrong, and the next libfprint upgrade silently overwrites it. For a
machine that is being used rather than worked on, build the package instead.

Building it as a package
------------------------

PKGBUILD builds libfprint-xiaomi-piano: the distribution's libfprint with this
one driver added. It replaces and conflicts with libfprint, and provides both
libfprint and libfprint-2.so, so fprintd is satisfied and pacman stops trying
to overwrite the library.

    makepkg -f                 # not as root; the driver sources are read from
    sudo pacman -U *.pkg.tar.zst   # this directory

It builds the -Ddrivers=default set, so every reader the distribution supports
still works, and it installs the same files the distribution's package does
apart from the gtk-doc HTML, which needs gtk-doc and is documentation only.

A new upstream libfprint will not be installed on its own any more: nothing
pulls in a package that something else already provides. Bump pkgver, refresh
the sha256sum, and rebuild when you want one - and be ready for the two
grafting points in prepare() to have moved.

How libfprint finds it
----------------------

It cannot, on its own. libfprint knows USB devices, udev devices that are
spidev or hidraw nodes, and virtual devices; this reader is a misc character
device and is none of those. So the driver declares itself virtual and is found
by the environment variable FP_XIAOMI_PIANO, the way libfprint finds anything
it cannot enumerate (fp-context.c, around the g_getenv call).

The cleaner answer is a new udev subtype - two places in fp-context.c and two
in fpi-device.c - which would let the node be matched directly. It is not done
here, and it would not remove the need for the service drop-in below.

The service drop-in
-------------------

/etc/systemd/system/fprintd.service.d/xiaomi-piano.conf carries three things
that are each load-bearing:

  Environment=FP_XIAOMI_PIANO=1     how the device is found at all

  DeviceAllow=/dev/tee0 rw          fprintd ships a device allow-list holding
  DeviceAllow=char-uio rw           rtc, hidraw, spi and usb. Neither of these
                                    is on it, and without them opening fails
                                    with EPERM - while ls shows permissions
                                    that look perfectly fine. The UIO node is
                                    allowed by class, since its minor is not
                                    fixed.

  ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/xiaomi-fingerprint
                                    ProtectSystem=strict otherwise stops the
                                    driver dropping the stale detect baseline.

Two behaviours worth knowing
----------------------------

The application must never be unloaded. Unloading it is accepted, and so is
loading it again, and then the first command sent to the new copy is refused
for the rest of the boot. The driver therefore drops only its own references
when it is done and leaves the application resident; loading it again over a
copy that is still there works, and hands back a usable controller. Nothing
short of a reboot undoes an unload, so if the sensor starts refusing to open,
look for something that called app_unload().

Leaving it loaded costs a resident application and nothing else. The sensor's
rail and its interrupt are not held by it - they are released through the
ioctls in gfpia_close() - so the sensor still powers down when fprintd exits
on idle.

Messages from this driver reach the journal on both streams, and stdout is
block-buffered there: everything written to it appears at once when the
process exits, interleaved wrongly with the stderr lines around it. Read the
order with that in mind, or write to stderr.

The sensor's detect baseline goes stale. gf_fdt_base.so is a calibration
snapshot, and a bad one stops the sensor reporting fingers at all: startup and
its reset interrupts still work, touching it does nothing, and nothing anywhere
reports an error. The application rewrites the file on every initialisation,
which makes its timestamp look fresh while the contents stay wrong, and it only
builds a new baseline when the file is absent. The driver therefore removes it
before initialising. If fingerprints ever stop being noticed, that file is the
first thing to delete.
